<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manasses Chronicle</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1340s</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Manasses Chronicle is a verse world history written by the Byzantine author Constantine Manasses in the twelfth century. Its Middle Bulgarian translation, made for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> Ivan Alexander in the 1340s, survives in an illuminated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatican-library">Vatican Library</a> with 69 miniatures, including scenes from Bulgarian history. It is one of the most important works of medieval Bulgarian art.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manasses-chronicle.jpg" alt="Manasses Chronicle" width="320" height="454" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Constantine Manasses · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:01-manasses-chronicle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
